; The Cellar - the map the board loads, and the whole of B2's point: THIS FILE IS THE MAP.
; Move a wall, resize the room, turn a floor into rubble, save, run. No code, no .tscn, no editor.
;
; DOUBLE RESOLUTION (EDGE_WALLS.md). The walls are drawn ON THE LINES between squares, because that
; is where a wall is on a wet-erase battle map - and the squares on BOTH sides of one are ordinary
; floor you can stand on. So the picture below is twice the size of the grid it describes: squares
; sit where a line and a column are both odd, and everything else is the lines between them.
;
;   .  floor                        |  wall, up a line between two columns
;   ~  difficult ground, costs 2    -  wall, along a line between two rows
;   #  rock - not part of the map   x  door, shut (which way it faces comes from where it is)
;   @  where the hero starts        +  a corner. drawn for the eye, parsed for nothing
;
; MapReader.Legend states that properly and a glyph in the wrong kind of position prints it at you,
; with the line and column - which is the mistake this format makes likely and the one it catches.
;
; Two rules worth knowing before you edit:
;   - a SPACE is a real glyph on a line position: it means "no wall there".
;   - the first line sets the width, so keep a '+' at every corner including the ends of the top
;     and bottom lines. Short lines are padded with open; long ones are refused by name.
;
; The rows read top to bottom as FAR SIDE OF THE TABLE first, so this is what you see from your
; seat. What is drawn, and why each part is here:
;
;   the hall, west          the whole left half, one open room, with a rock outcrop to walk AROUND
;   the wall down the middle  a THIN wall on one line: you can stand against it on either side,
;                           which is the entire reason this format exists
;   the door in it          shut, so the east half is unreachable until it is forced. B4 throws
;                           the hero's own pool at it; a pass opens it cleanly, a fail gets you
;                           through anyway and leaves the wreckage behind
;   the spill in the hall   difficult ground, double cost - a route crosses it when that is
;                           genuinely shorter and goes round when it is not
;   the two east chambers   split by a wall with a gap at the far end, so the way between them is
;                           the long way round
;
; Phase P moves maps like this inside a campaign package (ARCHITECTURE.md 4). Until then it lives
; beside the game and the loader reads exactly one of them, named on the Board node.
;
; NOTE FOR THE FIRST EXPORT: this is not a Godot resource, so an export preset has to list
; maps/*.map under "filters to export non-resource files" or it will not travel into the .pck.

